# Local setup: copy this file to .env, then run npm.cmd install
# and npm.cmd run dev on Windows (npm install / npm run dev elsewhere).
# Open http://localhost:3010. Scriber uses a separate local origin so another
# app's cached PWA on port 3000 cannot replace it. Dev startup fails if 3010 is busy.
# Device-only writing from an older origin stays there; it is not deleted or migrated.
# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for personalized quote and manifestation generation.
# Without it, AI endpoints return a clear 503; curated quotes and offline templates still work.
# Set your key locally from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey. Never paste it into chat or commit .env.
# AI Studio can inject this at runtime from its Secrets panel.
GEMINI_API_KEY=""
# Gemini 2.5 Flash is unavailable to new accounts; use the current Flash model.
GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-3.8-flash"
# If Flash is experiencing high demand, explicitly select gemini-3.1-flash-lite.
# Models are never silently switched per request; availability and quotas vary.

# Production: npm.cmd run build, then npm.cmd start (requires Node.js 22+).
# Both frontend assets and /api are served by the production server.
# npm.cmd run preview previews static assets only, not the backend.
# Render: connect this repository using New > Blueprint and its render.yaml.
# The Blueprint selects a free Node web service, installs from bun.lock, builds
# both frontend and backend, and starts the production server on Render's PORT.
# Supply GEMINI_API_KEY privately when Render prompts; do not add it to YAML.
# CLI setup can instead upload .env as a private Render secret file; dotenv
# loads it at runtime. Never upload it as a repository or frontend asset.
# Render uses gemini-3.1-flash-lite and three verified proxy hops on its onrender.com domain.
# Reverify proxy depth if another proxy or custom hosting setup is added.
# Automatic Git deploys require connecting GitHub to Render; a public clone
# without that connection needs manual deploys to pick up new commits.
# Authorize the final onrender.com hostname in Firebase Authentication before
# testing Google sign-in. Existing Firebase rules/database remain unchanged.
# Free services sleep after inactivity; the first visit or an AI request from
# an already-cached PWA can be slow or time out while the service wakes.
# Retry after the service is awake. Do not select a paid plan without approval.
PORT="3010"
HOST="0.0.0.0"
# Keep 0 locally. Set this only to the verified number of trusted reverse-proxy hops.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS="0"

# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.
# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.
# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.
APP_URL="http://localhost:3010"

# Firebase: Google is the only account provider. Enable it in Firebase Authentication
# and authorize localhost / 127.0.0.1 locally, plus the deployed app domain.
# Verify real Google sign-in at http://localhost:3010 in Chrome or Edge.
# Firebase Console login is separate from Scriber login. The editor's browser
# can have isolated storage and does not inherit a regular browser's account;
# use a regular browser if the editor blocks or loses the Google sign-in popup.
# Configuration lives in firebase-applet-config.json; production uses its named database.
# Deploy the updated client and rules together. firebase.json targets only Scriber's
# named database, not other databases in the same project.
# Sign in with an account authorized for that project:
# npm.cmd exec -- firebase login
# npm.cmd exec -- firebase deploy --only firestore:rules --project natural-land-slxdt
# Normal CLI deployment also requires serviceusage.services.use on the project
# (roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageConsumer), in addition to Firebase Rules permissions.
#
# Local verification (Java 21+ required):
# npm.cmd test
# npm.cmd run test:firebase
# To test the UI without real accounts: npm.cmd run emulators in one terminal;
# set VITE_USE_FIREBASE_EMULATORS=true in .env.local and run the dev server in another.
# This flag is honored ONLY in development. Demo accounts never access production data.
VITE_USE_FIREBASE_EMULATORS="false"
#
# Signed-in profiles, preferences, generated quotes, bookmarks, drafts and saved
# manifestations are owner-scoped. Pending writes stay in an account-scoped local
# queue, retry on reconnect/focus, and can be retried from Profile > Sync now.
# Guest data is separate; Profile's confirmed import copies only guest saved quotes,
# manifestations and an empty account draft. Guest originals and profile/themes stay put.
# Cloud manifestations allow 100000 writing characters per document; larger local
# writing stays on the device and must be split before cloud syncing.
# Device quota failures are reported; unsaved queue changes stay in memory while
# the page remains open. Free up storage and retry before closing the page.
# Photos are resized to 256x256 JPEG and kept under 200000 data-URL characters.
# Signing out hides account data but retains its offline cache on this device.
# Tabs, sheets and Firebase are loaded in chunks; every local chunk is precached
# for offline PWA use. The large Firestore vendor chunk is deferred until after
# the welcome screen renders; it may still trigger Vite's 500 kB vendor warning.
